{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"A\":\"Research a sparse-obstacle exact algorithm: factorial/inverse-factorial binomials modulo MOD, coordinate-sort blocked cells plus destination, and inclusion-exclusion over reachable prior blocked cells. Define edge cases (blocked start/end, duplicates) and assess practical O(M²) limits; propose a hybrid threshold rather than assuming it always wins.\",\"B\":\"Research an exact dense-grid path DP replacement for the current O(n²) code: minimize allocation/cache costs and evaluate safe OpenMP wavefront or row-prefix-scan parallelization. Preserve modulo arithmetic and all blocked-cell semantics; focus on regimes where sparse inclusion-exclusion is unattractive.\",\"C\":\"Independently investigate a correctness-preserving hybrid strategy and any viable subquadratic sparse-point method or pruning/order optimization for the blocked-cell recurrence. Compare asymptotic and expected costs using the observed 0.07984 s baseline as the only performance datum; do not specialize to hidden inputs.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only candidates that compile and pass full correctness checks. Benchmark each standalone and hybrid variant repeatedly against the baseline; select the fastest measured valid program, since combined ideas may regress. Retain robust input handling and standard output only.\",\"analysis\":\"Target is Problem D. The supplied implementation uses full n-by-n traversal with rolling rows, despite sparse blocked cells and n<200000; its measured valid baseline mean is 0.0798407 s on this run. Main research split is sparse combinatorial inclusion-exclusion, dense/parallel DP, and a rigorously chosen hybrid. All approaches must remain exact modulo 1,000,000,007 for every permitted input.\"}"}}
